  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_Column_Deleted" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Deleted</value>
    <comment>[header] Column heading in the cleanup result dialog, over the per-item sizes, after a run that deleted files permanently. It must read as unrecoverable, unlike the 'Moved' heading used for Recycle Bin runs. Narrow column (about 90 px).</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_Column_EstimatedSize" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Est. size</value>
    <comment>[header] Column heading in the cleanup result dialog, over the per-item sizes, after a dry run. 'Est.' is short for 'estimated'; the column is about 90 px wide, so keep it short.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_Column_Item" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Item</value>
    <comment>[header] Column heading in the cleanup result dialog, over the names of the cleaned-up suggestions.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_Column_Moved" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Moved</value>
    <comment>[header] Column heading in the cleanup result dialog, over the per-item sizes, after a run that sent files to the Recycle Bin. It must not read as if the files were destroyed. Narrow column (about 90 px).</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_Column_Status" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Status</value>
    <comment>[header] Column heading in the cleanup result dialog, over the per-item outcome (succeeded, partial, failed, skipped). Column is about 100 px wide.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_DryRun_PreviewFailed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Dry Run Report — Preview failed</value>
    <comment>[dialog] Title of the cleanup result dialog after a dry run that produced no results at all. Nothing was deleted and deletion stays blocked.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_DryRun_PreviewIncomplete" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Dry Run Report — Preview incomplete</value>
    <comment>[dialog] Title of the cleanup result dialog after a dry run that partly failed. Deletion stays blocked until the preview is clean.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_DryRun_ReadyForReview" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Dry Run Report — Ready for deletion review</value>
    <comment>[dialog] Title of the cleanup result dialog after a clean dry run. This is the one state in which the dialog also offers the two delete buttons, so it must not sound like anything has been deleted yet.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_NoOutcome" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Cleanup Report — No confirmed outcome</value>
    <comment>[dialog] Title of the cleanup result dialog after a real run that reported nothing back, so it is unknown what was removed.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_OneTargetFailed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>1 target failed: {0}</value>
    <comment>[safety] Small red note under an item in the cleanup result dialog when exactly one path could not be processed. {0} is that file or folder path.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_PartialOrFailed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Cleanup Report — Partial or failed outcome</value>
    <comment>[dialog] Title of the cleanup result dialog after a real run in which some items failed or were skipped.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_PermanentComplete" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Cleanup Report — Permanent deletion complete</value>
    <comment>[dialog] Title of the cleanup result dialog after a successful run that deleted the files permanently. The user must be able to tell from the title alone that the files are gone, unlike the Recycle Bin title.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_RecycleBinComplete" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Cleanup Report — Recycle Bin move complete</value>
    <comment>[dialog] Title of the cleanup result dialog after a successful run that moved the files to the Recycle Bin. The user must be able to tell from the title alone that the files are still recoverable, unlike the permanent-deletion title.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_TargetsFailed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>{0} targets failed; first: {1}</value>
    <comment>[safety] Small red note under an item in the cleanup result dialog when several paths could not be processed. {0} is how many failed, {1} is the first failing path.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_ReviewNotice_Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Analysis is read-only. Execution keeps Recycle Bin as the recommended path and dry-run can preview the exact selected categories.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Body of the information banner at the top of the Cleanup page. Explains that analysing changes nothing, that the Recycle Bin is the recommended (recoverable) deletion path, and that a dry run can preview the result first. Do not shorten or soften the Recycle Bin wording.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_ReviewNotice_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Review before delete</value>
    <comment>[safety] Title of the standing information banner at the top of the Cleanup page, above the options. Reminds the user to check the list before deleting.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Summary_Counts" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>({0} succeeded, {1} partial, {2} failed, {3} skipped)</value>
    <comment>[safety] Bracketed counts appended to the cleanup summary sentence on the Cleanup page, after a single space and followed by a full stop. {0} fully succeeded, {1} partly succeeded, {2} failed, {3} were skipped.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Summary_Permanent" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Permanently deleted {0} of logical file data; actual reclaimed disk allocation may differ</value>
    <comment>[safety] First half of the summary sentence shown on the Cleanup page and at the top of the result dialog after a run that deleted permanently. {0} is a formatted size such as '1.2 GB'. The counts sentence and a full stop are appended after it, so this text ends without punctuation. The user must be able to tell this apart from the Recycle Bin summary.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Summary_Preview" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Preview processed about {0} of logical file data ({1} succeeded, {2} partial, {3} failed, {4} skipped).</value>
    <comment>[safety] Summary sentence shown on the Cleanup page and at the top of the result dialog after a dry run. {0} is a formatted size such as '1.2 GB'; {1}-{4} are counts of items that fully succeeded, partly succeeded, failed and were skipped. 'Logical file data' is the size the files report, not the space actually reclaimed.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Summary_RecycleBin" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Moved {0} to the Recycle Bin; disk space remains in use until it is emptied</value>
    <comment>[safety] First half of the summary sentence shown on the Cleanup page and at the top of the result dialog after a run that used the Recycle Bin. {0} is a formatted size such as '1.2 GB'. The counts sentence and a full stop are appended after it, so this text ends without punctuation. The user must be able to tell this apart from the permanent-deletion summary.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Summary_ResolveFailures" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Resolve preview failures before deletion is enabled.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Sentence appended to the dry-run summary on the Cleanup page when the preview was not clean. It tells the user deletion stays blocked until the preview succeeds, so it must not sound optional.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_UseRecycleBin_Header" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Send to Recycle Bin (recommended)</value>
    <comment>[safety] Label of the toggle switch on the Cleanup page that chooses the recoverable deletion path. On = files go to the Windows Recycle Bin; off = they are deleted permanently. This is the single control that decides recoverable vs. unrecoverable, so it must never read as if it could mean permanent deletion.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_ClearDownloads_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Removes ALL content from {0}. This includes documents, archives, media, and any other files you may have downloaded. Estimated savings: {1}. This action cannot be easily undone — use Recycle Bin mode.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Warning paragraph under the 'clear entire Downloads folder' suggestion — the last thing the user reads before selecting a destructive action. {0} is the absolute Downloads path, {1} is an already-formatted size. 'ALL' is capitalised deliberately for emphasis; keep the emphasis and the undo warning at full strength. 'Recycle Bin mode' is the app's deletion mode that sends files to the Windows Recycle Bin.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_ClearDownloads_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Clear entire Downloads folder ({0} files)</value>
    <comment>[safety] Headline of the opt-in destructive cleanup suggestion that removes everything from the Downloads folder, not only installers. Appears in the Cleanup page list next to a risk badge. {0} is an already-grouped file count such as '3,417'. Do not soften 'entire'.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Database_CompactFailed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Could not compact the database. No scan data was removed.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Error banner after a failed attempt to rebuild StorageMaster's own database file. The second sentence reassures the user that no stored scan data was lost — keep that reassurance explicit. The technical detail is appended after this text in English.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_DeleteFile_Error_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>"{0}" was not moved to the Recycle Bin.</value>
    <comment>[safety] First line of the error dialog shown when moving a file to the Recycle Bin failed. {0} is the file name. The reason follows on its own line and is either the technical text from the deletion service (English) or Safety_DeleteFile_Error_Fallback. Must stay unambiguous that nothing was deleted.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_DeleteFile_Error_Fallback" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The file may be locked or access was denied.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Reason line in the failed-deletion dialog, used when the deletion service gave no reason of its own. 'locked' means another program is holding the file open.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_DeleteFile_Error_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Could not delete file</value>
    <comment>[safety] Title of the error dialog shown when moving a file to the Recycle Bin from the Results page failed. The file is still on disk.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_DeleteFile_Warning_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>File moved with a bookkeeping warning</value>
    <comment>[safety] Title of a dialog shown after a file was successfully moved to the Recycle Bin but the app could not fully update its own records of it. The file itself is fine; only the app's bookkeeping is incomplete. The detail below comes from the deletion service and stays English.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_DeleteScanFromHistory" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Delete this scan from history</value>
    <comment>[safety] Tooltip and screen-reader name of the trash-can button in the Results page header. Removes this scan's stored record from the app's history; it does not delete any file on disk. Keep that distinction clear so nobody reads it as deleting the scanned files.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_DeleteScan_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Permanently remove scan of "{0}" ({1}) from history?</value>
    <comment>[safety] Body of the confirmation dialog for removing a scan from the app's history. {0} is the scanned folder or drive path, {1} the scan's date. The sentence 'This cannot be undone.' (Safety_CannotBeUndone) is appended on its own line after this text, so do not repeat it here. Do not soften 'permanently'.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_DeleteScan_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Delete this scan?</value>
    <comment>[safety] Title of the confirmation dialog shown before a scan record is removed from the app's history. No user file is deleted by this action, only the stored scan.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_ConfirmDelete_Action" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Delete via {0}</value>
    <comment>[dialog] Label of the confirming button in that dialog - the one that actually deletes. {0} is the deletion route (Safety_Duplicates_Method_Quarantine / _RecycleBin / _Permanent), so the button reads for example 'Delete via Recycle Bin' or 'Delete via permanent deletion'. Naming the route on the button itself is deliberate: it is the user's last chance to notice that deletion is permanent.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_ConfirmDelete_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Delete {0} duplicate file(s) from page {1}?

One keeper per group stays untouched. Method: {2}.</value>
    <comment>[dialog] Body of the confirmation dialog shown before duplicates are deleted. {0} is an already-formatted count of files, {1} the page number of the group list, {2} the deletion route (Safety_Duplicates_Method_Quarantine / _RecycleBin / _Permanent). The blank line between the question and the reassurance must be kept. The second sentence promises that exactly one file in every group survives - do not weaken or drop it, and never make this dialog shorter or friendlier than the English.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_ConfirmDelete_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Delete selected duplicates from current page</value>
    <comment>[dialog] Title of the confirmation dialog shown before any duplicate is deleted. Says plainly what is about to happen and that it is limited to the page of groups currently on screen. Its body is Safety_Duplicates_ConfirmDelete_Body.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_DeleteMode_Permanent" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Delete mode: permanent delete.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Helper line under the delete-mode switches in Advanced options, shown when neither quarantine nor the Recycle Bin is chosen. This is the unrecoverable route - the wording must be unmistakably stronger than the Recycle Bin one, using the target language's Windows term for permanent deletion.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_DeleteMode_Quarantine" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Delete mode: quarantine. Files can be restored below.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Helper line under the delete-mode switches in Advanced options, shown while quarantine is chosen. Tells the user deleted duplicates are moved to quarantine and can be put back from the list further down the same page. Never drop the second sentence.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_DeleteMode_RecycleBin" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Delete mode: Recycle Bin.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Helper line under the delete-mode switches in Advanced options, shown while the Recycle Bin is chosen: deleted duplicates go to the Windows Recycle Bin and can be recovered from there. Use the exact Windows term for the Recycle Bin.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_DeleteSelected" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Delete selected</value>
    <comment>[safety] Primary destructive toolbar button on the Duplicates page. Deletes every file ticked on the current page of duplicate groups, using the delete mode chosen in Advanced options (quarantine, Recycle Bin or permanent). A confirmation dialog always follows; this label must read as an action, not a question.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_DeletingProgress" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Deleting duplicates… {0} of {1} group(s) processed.</value>
    <comment>[status] Status line at the bottom of the Duplicates page while a deletion is running. {0} is the number of groups already handled and {1} the total, both already formatted. Ends with a horizontal-ellipsis character in English.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_InfoBar_Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Exact matches can be batch-selected. Fuzzy text, image, and video matches require review; quarantine remains preferred when restore metadata exists.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Body of the safety banner at the top of the Duplicates page. Three claims, all of which must survive translation intact: (1) exact (byte-identical) matches are safe to select in bulk; (2) approximate ('fuzzy') matches of text documents, images and video are NOT, and each must be looked at by the user before deletion; (3) quarantine - a reversible move to a holding area that records where the file came from - is the preferred deletion route whenever a restore record can be written. Never shorten or soften this: it is what stops a user deleting files that only look alike.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_InfoBar_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Safety first</value>
    <comment>[safety] Title of the blue information banner pinned at the top of the Duplicates page, above everything else. Introduces Safety_Duplicates_InfoBar_Message, which explains which matches may be bulk-selected for deletion and which must be reviewed one by one. Short headline; do not weaken it.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_Method_Permanent" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>permanent deletion</value>
    <comment>[safety] Name of the deletion route, used inside two longer sentences on the Duplicates page: as {2} of Safety_Duplicates_ConfirmDelete_Body and as {0} of Safety_Duplicates_ConfirmDelete_Action ('Delete via permanent deletion'). This is the unrecoverable route and must read as such - never as a plain word for 'delete'.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_Method_Quarantine" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>quarantine (restorable)</value>
    <comment>[safety] Name of the deletion route, used inside two longer sentences on the Duplicates page: as {2} of Safety_Duplicates_ConfirmDelete_Body ('Method: quarantine (restorable).') and as {0} of Safety_Duplicates_ConfirmDelete_Action ('Delete via quarantine (restorable)'). Lower-case because it appears mid-sentence in English; capitalise as the target language requires. The bracketed word is the user's assurance that the files can be brought back.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_Method_RecycleBin" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Recycle Bin</value>
    <comment>[safety] Name of the deletion route, used inside two longer sentences on the Duplicates page: as {2} of Safety_Duplicates_ConfirmDelete_Body and as {0} of Safety_Duplicates_ConfirmDelete_Action ('Delete via Recycle Bin'). Use the exact Windows term.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_Outcome_Permanent" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Permanently deleted {0} across {1} file(s)</value>
    <comment>[safety] First half of the status line reported after duplicates were deleted permanently, with no way back. {0} is an already-formatted size, {1} an already-formatted file count. A sentence fragment continued by Duplicates_Status_DeleteDone and its variants - do not add a full stop. Must clearly say the deletion was permanent.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_Outcome_Quarantine" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Moved {0} across {1} file(s) to quarantine</value>
    <comment>[safety] First half of the status line reported after duplicates were deleted with the quarantine route. {0} is an already-formatted size such as '820 MB', {1} an already-formatted file count. It is a sentence fragment: it is inserted as {0} into Duplicates_Status_DeleteDone and its warning/error variants, which append 'from page 3.' or '; 2 group(s) failed...'. Do not add a full stop.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_Outcome_RecycleBin" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Moved {0} across {1} file(s) to the Recycle Bin</value>
    <comment>[safety] First half of the status line reported after duplicates were deleted to the Windows Recycle Bin. {0} is an already-formatted size, {1} an already-formatted file count. A sentence fragment continued by Duplicates_Status_DeleteDone and its variants - do not add a full stop. Use the exact Windows term for the Recycle Bin.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_QuarantineSource_Cleanup" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>· Cleanup quarantine</value>
    <comment>[label] Small muted tag on a quarantine-list row saying the file was quarantined by a Cleanup-page rule rather than by duplicate deletion. The leading middle dot is a separator from the timestamp printed before it - keep it and the space that follows.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_QuarantineSource_Duplicate" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>· Duplicate quarantine</value>
    <comment>[label] Small muted tag on a quarantine-list row saying the file was quarantined by a duplicate deletion run rather than by a cleanup rule. The leading middle dot is a separator from the timestamp printed before it - keep it and the space that follows.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_Quarantine_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Includes duplicate-run quarantines and generic cleanup quarantines.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Muted line under the quarantine panel heading on the Duplicates page. Says the list is not limited to files quarantined by duplicate deletion: files quarantined by the Cleanup page appear here too.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_Quarantine_Header" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>All quarantined files — restorable</value>
    <comment>[safety] Bold heading of the panel at the bottom right of the Duplicates page listing every file currently sitting in quarantine. The part after the dash is a promise to the user: each of these files can still be put back where it came from. Keep that reassurance; the character between the halves is an em dash.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_Restore" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Restore</value>
    <comment>[safety] Button at the end of each row in the quarantine list on the Duplicates page. Moves that one file out of quarantine and back to its original location. Narrow button - keep it short, and use the glossary term for Restore.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_RestoreFailed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Restore failed: {0}</value>
    <comment>[status] Status line after restoring a quarantined file did not work. {0} is the underlying technical error message, which stays in English. Only the leading words are translated.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_RestoreFailed_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Could not restore "{0}".

{1}</value>
    <comment>[dialog] Body of that error dialog. {0} is the file's original full path, shown in quotation marks; {1} is the underlying technical error message and stays in English. Keep the blank line between the sentence and the technical detail, and use the target language's quotation marks around {0}.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_RestoreFailed_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Restore failed</value>
    <comment>[dialog] Title of the error dialog shown when a quarantined file could not be put back. Its body is Safety_Duplicates_RestoreFailed_Body.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_RestoreSucceeded" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Restored {0}.</value>
    <comment>[status] Status line after the user pressed Restore on a quarantined file and it was put back. {0} is the file's original full path.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_UseQuarantine" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Use quarantine</value>
    <comment>[safety] Label on the on/off switch that moves deleted duplicates into StorageMaster's own quarantine folder, from which each file can be restored to its original location. Mutually exclusive with 'Use Recycle Bin'; when both are off, deletion is permanent and unrecoverable.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_UseRecycleBin" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Use Recycle Bin</value>
    <comment>[safety] Label on the on/off switch that sends deleted duplicates to the Windows Recycle Bin instead of removing them permanently. Mutually exclusive with 'Use quarantine'; when both are off, deletion is permanent and unrecoverable. Use the exact Windows term for the Recycle Bin.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_MoveToRecycleBin" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Move to Recycle Bin</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_PermanentDelete" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Permanently delete</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_ProgramLeftovers_Notes" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Heuristic only. Not proof of uninstallation. Review path and restore from Recycle Bin if needed.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Safety note shown in its own line beneath a program-leftover suggestion on the Cleanup page. It warns that the detection can be wrong and reminds the user that these deletions go to the Recycle Bin and can be restored. Keep all three statements; do not shorten.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_RecycleBin_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Recycle Bin currently holds {0} across {1} items. Emptying it is irreversible.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Explanatory paragraph under the empty-Recycle-Bin cleanup suggestion. {0} is an already-formatted size such as '6.8 GB', {1} is an already-grouped item count. The second sentence is the safety statement: emptying the bin permanently destroys the files, so state it just as plainly as the English does.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_RecycleBin_Notes" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Permanent deletion only. Emptying the Recycle Bin cannot be undone.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Safety note shown in its own line beneath the empty-Recycle-Bin suggestion on the Cleanup page. It states that this suggestion cannot be run in Recycle Bin or quarantine mode — the files go for good. Must not be softened or shortened.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_ReviewDuplicatesBeforeDeleting" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Review duplicates before deleting anything large or old.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Standing advice always listed last in the Dashboard "Recommended actions" list. Warns the user to inspect duplicate groups before deleting files. Must not be softened: it exists to stop unchecked deletion.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_ScanHistory_PurgeStopped" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>History purge stopped. Some sessions may already have been deleted; refresh scan history before retrying.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Error banner in Settings when purging old scan history failed partway through. Warns that the removal was partial and already-deleted sessions are gone. The technical detail is appended after this text in English.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_ScanHistory_SessionsDeleted" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Deleted {0} old scan session(s). Compact the database to release the space on disk.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Confirmation banner in Settings after purging scan history older than the retention window. {0} is how many stored scan sessions were removed; the removal is permanent. The second sentence points at the separate Compact database button, which is what actually shrinks the file. English uses "(s)" to cover 1 and many.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Scheduler_CleanupConsentRequired" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enabled scheduled cleanup requires current consent for its exact target, rules, schedule, and Recycle Bin safety policy.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Warning banner in the Scheduling section of Settings, shown instead of saving when the user tries to enable a scheduled job that deletes files without having confirmed that exact combination of target folder, cleanup rules, schedule and Recycle Bin setting. Do not shorten or soften it.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Scheduler_DeleteFailed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Scheduled job deletion failed; prior task/settings state was restored where possible.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Error banner in the Scheduling section of Settings when the scheduled job could not be removed and the previous state was rolled back, so the job still exists. The technical detail is appended after this text in English.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Scheduler_DeletedRefreshFailed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Scheduled job was deleted, but the editor refresh failed. Refresh before another change.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Warning banner in the Scheduling section of Settings: the job really was removed, but the on-screen list is now stale and must be reloaded before editing again. The technical detail is appended after this text in English.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Scheduler_JobDeleted" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Scheduled job deleted.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Short confirmation banner in the Scheduling section of Settings after the selected scheduled job was removed from Windows Task Scheduler.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_SendToRecycleBin" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Send to Recycle Bin</value>
    <comment>[safety] Action that moves the selected file to the Windows Recycle Bin, from where it can still be restored. Used as a right-click menu entry, as the tooltip and screen-reader name of the delete button on a file row in Results, and as the confirming button of the delete dialog. Must clearly mean the recoverable Recycle Bin move, never permanent deletion.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_SendToRecycleBin_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Move "{0}" ({1}) to the Recycle Bin?</value>
    <comment>[safety] Body of the confirmation dialog for moving one file to the Windows Recycle Bin. {0} is the file name, {1} its size (already formatted, for example '1.4 GB'). Must clearly mean the recoverable move, never permanent deletion.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_SendToRecycleBin_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Send to Recycle Bin?</value>
    <comment>[safety] Title of the confirmation dialog shown before a file is moved to the Windows Recycle Bin. Must clearly mean the recoverable move, never permanent deletion.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Settings_CleanupWarning" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Cleanup permanently removes files. Enable Recycle Bin or dry-run until you are confident.</value>
    <comment>[safety] SAFETY. Information bar pinned at the top of the Cleanup and Safety settings category, above the deletion switches. Warns that cleanup destroys files and points at the two safety nets below it. Must not be softened or shortened; permanently must stay unmistakable.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Settings_ClearEntireDownloads_AutomationHelp" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Clears all files in Downloads, not just installer files. Requires the Downloaded Installers rule to be enabled.</value>
    <comment>[safety] SAFETY. Screen-reader help text for the Clear entire Downloads folder switch. The scope warning in the first sentence must not be softened. Downloaded Installers refers to the rule labelled Settings_Rule_DownloadedInstallers and must be translated the same way. Not shown on screen.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Settings_ClearEntireDownloads_Header" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Clear entire Downloads folder</value>
    <comment>[safety] SAFETY. Label of the on/off switch in Cleanup and Safety settings that widens the installer rule to delete everything in the user's Downloads folder, not only setup files. Also used as that switch's screen-reader name. Downloads is the Windows folder name.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Settings_DryRun_AutomationHelp" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Previews what would be deleted without actually removing anything.</value>
    <comment>[safety] SAFETY. Screen-reader help text for the dry-run switch in Cleanup and Safety settings. Not shown on screen.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Settings_DryRun_Header" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Dry-run mode by default</value>
    <comment>[safety] SAFETY. Label of the on/off switch in Cleanup and Safety settings that makes cleanup runs preview-only instead of deleting. Also used as that switch's screen-reader name.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Settings_FuzzyMatchWarning" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Perceptual matching can return false positives. Always review fuzzy results before deleting.</value>
    <comment>[safety] SAFETY. Yellow warning bar pinned at the top of the Duplicates and Matching settings category. Warns that similarity matching can wrongly group unrelated files, so the user must check before deleting. Must not be softened.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Settings_PreferRecycleBin_AutomationHelp" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Moves deleted files to the Recycle Bin so they can be restored. Turning this off enables permanent deletion.</value>
    <comment>[safety] SAFETY. Screen-reader help text for the Send to Recycle Bin switch in Cleanup and Safety settings. The second sentence is the warning about switching it off and must stay just as plain. Not shown on screen.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Settings_PreferRecycleBin_AutomationName" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Prefer Recycle Bin</value>
    <comment>[safety] SAFETY. Screen-reader name for the Send to Recycle Bin switch in Cleanup and Safety settings. Shorter than the visible label. Not shown on screen.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Settings_PreferRecycleBin_Header" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Send to Recycle Bin (recommended)</value>
    <comment>[safety] SAFETY. Label of the on/off switch in Cleanup and Safety settings that decides whether deleted files go to the Recycle Bin (recoverable) or are erased permanently. Also used as that switch's screen-reader name. Recycle Bin must use the exact Windows term.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Settings_PurgeHistory_AutomationHelp" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Permanently removes scan sessions older than the retention days.</value>
    <comment>[safety] SAFETY. Screen-reader help text for the delete-old-history button. Permanently must stay unmistakable; this data is not recoverable. Not shown on screen.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Settings_PurgeHistory_AutomationName" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Purge old scan history</value>
    <comment>[safety] SAFETY. Screen-reader name for the delete-old-history button in Results and History settings. Not shown on screen.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Settings_PurgeHistory_Button" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Delete Scan History Older Than Retention Window</value>
    <comment>[safety] SAFETY. Button in the Results and History settings category. Permanently removes stored scan sessions older than the retention period set by the slider above it. Names a destructive action, so the wording must stay explicit about what is deleted.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Settings_ScheduledCleanupDialog_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Job: {0}
Target: {1}
Rules: {2}
Schedule: {3}
Deletion method: Recycle Bin
Allowed risk: Safe or Low only
Clear entire Downloads folder: Disabled

StorageMaster will run this exact cleanup plan automatically. Any later plan change invalidates consent and blocks execution until you review and save it again. Disk allocation remains used until the Recycle Bin is emptied.</value>
    <comment>[safety] SAFETY. Full body of the unattended-cleanup confirmation dialog - the last thing the user reads before granting consent for automatic deletion. The first block is a labelled summary, one item per line: {0} job name, {1} target folder path, {2} comma-separated rule identifiers (stay English), {3} the schedule line built from Settings_Schedule_Weekly or Settings_Schedule_Daily. Keep every line break, keep the blank line before the closing paragraph, keep all four placeholders, and translate the labels before each colon. Recycle Bin must use the exact Windows term. Safe and Low are the app's risk-level names. The closing paragraph must not be shortened or softened.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Settings_ScheduledCleanupDialog_Confirm" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enable Scheduled Cleanup</value>
    <comment>[safety] SAFETY. Primary button of the unattended-cleanup confirmation dialog. Pressing it grants consent for automatic deletion. The other button uses Common_Cancel and is the default, so this one must stay clearly the deliberate choice.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Settings_ScheduledCleanupDialog_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enable Unattended Cleanup?</value>
    <comment>[safety] SAFETY. Title of the confirmation dialog shown when the user saves a scheduled job that will delete files without anyone present. It is a question, so Spanish needs the inverted opening mark. Do not make it friendlier than the English.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Settings_ScheduledCleanup_Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enabled cleanup jobs require dedicated confirmation. They rescan, revalidate targets, and move supported files to the Recycle Bin; allocation is reclaimed only after the bin is emptied.</value>
    <comment>[safety] SAFETY. Body of the warning bar at the top of Scheduling and Automation settings. Explains that scheduled cleanups need separate consent, that they move files to the Recycle Bin rather than erasing them, and that disk space is not freed until the bin is emptied. Recycle Bin must use the exact Windows term; none of the three points may be dropped.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Settings_ScheduledCleanup_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Scheduled cleanup runs unattended</value>
    <comment>[safety] SAFETY. Heading of the yellow warning bar pinned at the top of the Scheduling and Automation settings category. Warns that a scheduled cleanup deletes files with nobody watching.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Smart_CleanSelected_Button" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Clean Selected</value>
    <comment>[safety] The Smart Cleaner page's execute button, next to the selected total. Opens a confirmation dialog before anything is deleted.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Smart_Confirm_DeleteButton" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Delete selected</value>
    <comment>[safety] Confirm button of the Smart Cleaner confirmation dialog when the Recycle Bin switch is off. Pressing it destroys the selected junk without any way back; it must not read the same as the Recycle Bin variant.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Smart_Confirm_MoveButton" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Move selected</value>
    <comment>[safety] Confirm button of the Smart Cleaner confirmation dialog when the Recycle Bin switch is on. Pressing it moves the selected junk to the Recycle Bin, where it can still be restored.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Smart_Confirm_Permanent_Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Permanently delete {0} junk group(s), about {1}?

This cannot be undone.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Body of the Smart Cleaner confirmation dialog when the Recycle Bin switch is off, so the files are destroyed. {0} is the number of selected junk categories, {1} a formatted size such as '1.2 GB'. Keep the blank line between the question and the warning, and never soften 'cannot be undone'.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Smart_Confirm_RecycleBin_Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Move {0} junk group(s), about {1}, to the Recycle Bin?

Disk space is reclaimed only after the Recycle Bin is emptied.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Body of the Smart Cleaner confirmation dialog when the Recycle Bin switch is on. {0} is the number of selected junk categories, {1} a formatted size such as '1.2 GB'. Keep the blank line between the question and the note. The user must be able to tell this apart from the permanent-deletion message: this one is recoverable and frees no space until the Recycle Bin is emptied.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Smart_Confirm_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Confirm cleanup</value>
    <comment>[safety] Title of the confirmation dialog on the Smart Cleaner page, shown after the user presses 'Clean Selected' and before anything is removed.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Smart_ReadOnlyNotice" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Analysis is read-only. Nothing is removed until you select categories and confirm.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Helper text in the introduction card on the Smart Cleaner page. It is the page's standing safety notice: scanning changes nothing, and deletion needs an explicit selection plus a confirmation.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Smart_Summary_Permanent" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Permanently deleted {0}; deletion service reported about {1} reclaimed.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Sentence describing what a finished Smart Cleaner run did when files were deleted permanently. {0} is the size that was processed, {1} the size actually reclaimed, both already formatted such as '1.2 GB'. It is embedded in the longer status sentences (keys Smart_Status_Completed and its variants), so it has to read as a self-contained sentence. The user must be able to tell it apart from the Recycle Bin wording.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Smart_Summary_RecycleBin" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Moved {0} to the Recycle Bin. Disk space returns after the Recycle Bin is emptied.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Sentence describing what a finished Smart Cleaner run did when the Recycle Bin was used. {0} is a formatted size such as '1.2 GB'. It is embedded in the longer status sentences (keys Smart_Status_Completed and its variants), so it has to read as a self-contained sentence. The user must be able to tell it apart from the permanent-deletion wording.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Smart_UseRecycleBin_Header" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Send deletions to Recycle Bin (recommended)</value>
    <comment>[safety] Label of the toggle switch on the Smart Cleaner page that chooses the recoverable deletion path. On = files go to the Windows Recycle Bin; off = they are deleted permanently. This single control decides recoverable vs. unrecoverable, so it must never read as if it could mean permanent deletion.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_SpaceMap_ChildrenShown" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Showing {0} largest child item(s). Destructive actions route through cleanup or duplicate review.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Status line in the Space Map information bar after the treemap is drawn. First sentence: how many of the largest items inside the current folder are shown; {0} is an already-formatted count such as 1,250. Second sentence reassures the user that the Space Map itself never deletes anything - deleting only happens on the Cleanup or Duplicates review pages. Do not soften or drop the second sentence.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Summary_PermanentDeleteEnabled" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Permanent delete enabled</value>
    <comment>[safety] Status badge on the "Cleanup &amp; Safety" card on the Settings hub, shown when deleted files bypass the Recycle Bin and are erased for good. Must not be confusable with ordinary deletion.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Summary_RecycleBinPreferred" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Recycle Bin preferred</value>
    <comment>[safety] Status badge on the "Cleanup &amp; Safety" card on the Settings hub, shown when deleted files go to the Windows Recycle Bin and can be restored. Use the Windows term for the Recycle Bin.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Warning_PermanentDeletionRisky" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Permanent deletion is risky. Enable Recycle Bin for safety.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Warning on the "Cleanup &amp; Safety" card on the Settings hub, shown while permanent deletion is switched on. Do not soften it: files deleted this way cannot be recovered.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Admin_Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>All files and system folders are accessible.</value>
    <comment>[description] Body of the green information banner on the Scan page shown when the app already has Windows administrator rights.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Admin_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Running as Administrator</value>
    <comment>[title] Heading of the green information banner on the Scan page shown when the app already has Windows administrator rights.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_AutomationName" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Scan page</value>
    <comment>[accessibility] Screen-reader name announced for the whole Scan page when it opens. This is the page where the user picks what to scan and starts the scan.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Browse" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Browse…</value>
    <comment>[button] Button beside the path box on the Scan page that opens the Windows folder picker. Ends with an ellipsis character because it opens a dialog.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Calculating" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Calculating</value>
    <comment>[status] Placeholder shown in the Speed, File rate and ETA readouts of the live progress panel until enough of the scan has run to measure them.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Complete_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Scan complete. Workspace ready.</value>
    <comment>[title] Heading of the green banner at the bottom of the Scan page after a scan finishes. "Workspace" is the app page holding the scan results.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_DeepScan_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Attempts protected directories, disables the default system excludes, and records access-denied errors instead of silently skipping them. Administrator mode is recommended.</value>
    <comment>[description] Helper paragraph under the Deep Scan toggle on the Scan page, spelling out what deep scanning changes.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Elevation_Button" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Run as Administrator</value>
    <comment>[button] Button in the amber warning panel on the Scan page that restarts the scan in a separate process with Windows administrator rights.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Elevation_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Without admin rights, access-denied errors will be recorded for protected system folders. Use the button to relaunch with full privileges.</value>
    <comment>[description] Body of the amber warning panel on the Scan page, explaining what happens without administrator rights. "The button" is the Scan_Elevation_Button beside this text.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Elevation_Failed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Could not start the elevated scan worker.</value>
    <comment>[status] Error shown on the Scan page when the request for administrator rights was refused or the second process could not be started.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Elevation_Started" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Elevated deep scan started in a separate command-line worker. Refresh Results when it completes.</value>
    <comment>[status] Status line on the Scan page after the user chooses "Run as Administrator". The scan then runs in a second, elevated process, so this page cannot show its progress. "Results" names the app page the user should refresh.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Elevation_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Administrator privileges recommended</value>
    <comment>[title] Heading of the amber warning panel on the Scan page shown when deep scan is on but the app does not have Windows administrator rights.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Error_FolderPickerFailed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Could not open folder picker: {0}</value>
    <comment>[status] Error banner on the Scan page when the Windows folder-picker dialog could not be opened. {0} is the technical error detail, which stays in English by policy - translate only the sentence before it and keep the colon.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Error_InitializeFailed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Scan page failed to initialize: {0}</value>
    <comment>[status] Error banner on the Scan page when the page itself could not load its drives or settings. {0} is the technical error detail, which stays in English by policy - translate only the sentence before it and keep the colon.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Error_NoDetails" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The scanner reported a failure without additional details.</value>
    <comment>[status] Error text on the Scan page when the scan failed but gave no reason of its own.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Error_NonTerminalStatus" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The scanner returned non-terminal status {0}.</value>
    <comment>[status] Error text on the Scan page for an internal fault: the scanner came back while still claiming to be in progress. {0} is an untranslated status word.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Eta_Estimating" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Estimating…</value>
    <comment>[status] Value of the "ETA" readout in the live progress panel before the scan has run long enough to predict a finish time. Ends with an ellipsis character.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Eta_Finalizing" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Finalizing</value>
    <comment>[status] Value of the "ETA" readout once every expected byte has been read and the scan is writing its results.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Eta_NoSubtreeEstimate" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>No estimate for a subtree</value>
    <comment>[status] Value of the "ETA" readout when only part of a drive is being scanned. The app refuses to guess a finish time because it does not know how big that folder is. "Subtree" means a folder rather than a whole drive.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Failed_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Scan failed</value>
    <comment>[title] Heading of the red banner at the bottom of the Scan page when a scan ends in an error. The detail underneath comes from the scanner.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_FileRate_PerMinute" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>{0} files/min</value>
    <comment>[status] Value of the "File rate" readout in the live progress panel when the scan is processing less than one file per second. {0} is a formatted number. Keep the per-minute unit short - it sits in a narrow column.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_FileRate_PerSecond" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>{0} files/s</value>
    <comment>[status] Value of the "File rate" readout in the live progress panel at normal speeds. {0} is a formatted number. Keep the per-second unit short - it sits in a narrow column.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Metric_Elapsed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Elapsed</value>
    <comment>[label] Caption over the time the running scan has taken so far, in the live progress panel.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Metric_Errors" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Errors</value>
    <comment>[label] Caption over the count of files and folders the scan could not read, in the live progress panel shown while a scan runs.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Metric_Eta" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>ETA</value>
    <comment>[label] Caption over the estimated time still remaining, in the live progress panel. English uses the abbreviation; use whatever short form is normal in the target language.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Metric_FileRate" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>File rate</value>
    <comment>[label] Caption over how many files per second or per minute the scan is processing, in the live progress panel. Shown next to Speed because byte throughput alone is misleading for many small files.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Metric_Files" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Files</value>
    <comment>[label] Caption over the running count of files found so far, in the live progress panel shown while a scan runs.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Metric_Folders" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Folders</value>
    <comment>[label] Caption over the running count of folders visited so far, in the live progress panel shown while a scan runs.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Metric_Size" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Size</value>
    <comment>[label] Caption over the total size measured so far (for example "4.20 GB"), in the live progress panel shown while a scan runs.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Metric_Speed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Speed</value>
    <comment>[label] Caption over the current throughput in bytes per second, in the live progress panel shown while a scan runs.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Mode_Custom" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Custom Scan</value>
    <comment>[label] Title of the fourth of four clickable scan-mode tiles on the Scan page, and the heading shown over the options once that mode is picked. Uses whatever the user has already configured.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Mode_Custom_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Use current toggles and persisted exclusions.</value>
    <comment>[description] Explanation under the "Custom Scan" tile title on the Scan page. "Toggles" are the switches on this page; "exclusions" are folders the user has excluded in Settings.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Mode_Deep" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Deep Scan</value>
    <comment>[label] Name of the most thorough scan mode. Used in three places on the Scan page: the third of four mode tiles, the heading over the options once it is picked, and the label of the toggle that switches deep scanning on.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Mode_Deep_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Protected folders and access-denied reporting.</value>
    <comment>[description] Explanation under the "Deep Scan" tile title on the Scan page. Means the mode enters protected folders and reports what it could not read.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Mode_Quick" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Quick Scan</value>
    <comment>[label] Title of the first of four clickable scan-mode tiles on the Scan page, and the heading shown over the options once that mode is picked. The fastest mode.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Mode_Quick_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Fast metadata pass for common storage review.</value>
    <comment>[description] Explanation under the "Quick Scan" tile title on the Scan page.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Mode_Standard" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Standard Scan</value>
    <comment>[label] Title of the second of four clickable scan-mode tiles on the Scan page, and the heading shown over the options once that mode is picked. This is the default mode.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Mode_Standard_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Recommended balance for routine drive scans.</value>
    <comment>[description] Explanation under the "Standard Scan" tile title on the Scan page.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_PathError_DriveNotReady" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Selected drive is not ready.</value>
    <comment>[status] Validation message under the path box on the Scan page when the drive exists but cannot be read - for example an empty card reader or a disconnected mapped drive.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_PathError_Empty" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Choose a folder or drive to scan.</value>
    <comment>[status] Validation message under the path box on the Scan page when the box is empty. Blocks the Start Scan button.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_PathError_Missing" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Scan path does not exist or is not currently available.</value>
    <comment>[status] Validation message under the path box on the Scan page when the typed folder cannot be found - it may have been deleted, or sit on a disconnected network share.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_PathError_NotAbsolute" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Scan path must be an absolute Windows path.</value>
    <comment>[status] Validation message under the path box on the Scan page when the typed path is relative. An absolute path starts with a drive letter or a network share.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_PathError_PreflightFailed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Startup preflight failed: {0}</value>
    <comment>[status] Validation message under the path box on the Scan page when the app cannot write to its own data folder, so no scan could be saved. {0} is the technical error detail, which stays in English by policy - translate only the sentence before it and keep the colon.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Path_Placeholder" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>e.g. C:\ or C:\Users</value>
    <comment>[placeholder] Grey hint text inside the empty path box on the Scan page. The two Windows paths are examples and must stay exactly as they are; translate only the "e.g." part.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Progress_Cancelled" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Scan cancelled.</value>
    <comment>[status] Status line in the Scan page progress panel after the user stops a running scan. Has a closing full stop, unlike the Scan_Step_Cancelled wayfinding line.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Progress_Complete" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Scan complete — {0} in {1} files</value>
    <comment>[status] Status line summarising a finished scan, shown in the progress panel and repeated in the green completion banner. {0} is a formatted size such as "4.20 GB"; {1} is a formatted file count. The dash is an em dash. No closing full stop in English.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Progress_DidNotComplete" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Scan did not complete.</value>
    <comment>[status] Status line in the Scan page progress panel for the rare case where the scanner returns without either finishing, failing or being cancelled.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Progress_Failed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Scan failed.</value>
    <comment>[status] Status line in the Scan page progress panel when a scan ends in an error. Has a closing full stop, unlike the Scan_Step_Failed wayfinding line.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Progress_Preparing" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Preparing scan...</value>
    <comment>[status] Status line in the live progress panel between pressing Start Scan and the first file being counted. English uses three full stops rather than an ellipsis character.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Scan_Progress_Running" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>{0} scanned · {1} files</value>
